From 597eb14696322cef56de88444582832edb547114 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 22:55:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] doc/examples/decode_audio: flush the parser at the end of the
 input

av_parser_parse2() has to be called with a buffer size of 0 at the
end of the input so that the parser outputs the data it still holds.
decode_video.c does so, decode_audio.c never did.

For a complete MP2 file this makes no difference, as the MPEG audio
parser returns a frame as soon as all of its bytes have arrived. It
does when the file is cut in the middle of a frame: the incomplete
last frame was dropped, while ffmpeg decodes it. With the first 3000
bytes of a 128 kb/s stereo file the example wrote 32256 bytes, and

    ffmpeg -i short.mp2 -f s16le out.pcm

writes 36864 bytes.

Parsers that need to see the start of the next frame hold back more.
With the codec ID changed to AV_CODEC_ID_FLAC, the last 14 frames of
a 5 second file were missing from the output.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FZ4G6798n6Ua4qu6xwWu3S
---
 doc/examples/decode_audio.c | 14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/doc/examples/decode_audio.c b/doc/examples/decode_audio.c
index cb35e55..5f1b7cb 100644
--- a/doc/examples/decode_audio.c
+++ b/doc/examples/decode_audio.c
@@ -220,6 +220,20 @@ int main(int argc, char **argv)
         exit(1);
     }
 
+    /* flush the parser: a size of 0 signals the end of the input, so that
+     * the parser returns the data it still holds */
+    do {
+        ret = av_parser_parse2(parser, c, &pkt->data, &pkt->size,
+                               NULL, 0, AV_NOPTS_VALUE, AV_NOPTS_VALUE, 0);
+        if (ret < 0) {
+            fprintf(stderr, "Error while parsing\n");
+            exit(1);
+        }
+
+        if (pkt->size)
+            decode(c, pkt, decoded_frame, outfile);
+    } while (pkt->size);
+
     /* flush the decoder */
     pkt->data = NULL;
     pkt->size = 0;
-- 
